{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 120,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to DeleteMe for Supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2640,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Giant space rocks are definitely going to hit the \nEarth again. We actually do know how to deflect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them, but only if we find them and correctly \nassess their risk first. But the solar system is a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14600,
    "dDurationMs": 6773,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chaotic place. How is it even possible to tell if \na space rock will one day collide with the Earth?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27520,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey everyone! Before we dive in, a quick \nupdate on our experiment. A few episodes ago,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32680,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we asked for your help in an experiment to see \nif more likes, comments, and subscribers could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37760,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "give Space Time a boost in the algorithm—and yeah, \ndid you deliver some impressive datapoints! In  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46400,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first 24 hours of our latest episodes, we \nsaw over 8,000 comments (a 1043% increase) and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54880,
    "dDurationMs": 9480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "45,000 likes (up 275%). Overall this helped boost \nimpressions by 143%, spreading Space Time to more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64360,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the community. Even better? Many of you told us \nSpace Time is popping up on your homepages again!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70960,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Honestly, this has been the best 10-year \nanniversary gift we could ask for. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what’s next? Phase 2. This time, \nlet’s push even further. Our goal:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82680,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "50,000+ likes in 24 hours and a thriving \ncomment section. That means asking questions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "replying to others, and keeping the conversation \ngoing. We will track the results and report back  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "soon! And if you haven’t seen yet, we’ve \ngot special 10-year anniversary merch to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99680,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "celebrate a decade of breaking brains—check it \nout at the merch store! Now, let’s get into it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105560,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To you this may look like a moving smudge. \nBut it’s actually the asteroid 2024 YR4,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112240,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discovered by the Asteroid Terrestrial-impact Last \nAlert System (ATLAS). Based on early observations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119080,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NASA calculated a 3.1% chance of this striking \nthe Earth on the 22nd of December, 2032."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126200,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since then, further observations have \nlowered that impact probability to close  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129720,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to zero. essentially eliminated the threat \nLow enough that we don’t need to scramble our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132960,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spaceship full of oil rig workers, and also \nlow enough that this isn’t an episode about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138520,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether 2024 YR4 will hit us. Rather, this is an \nepisode about how it’s even possible to go from a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146760,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fuzzy dot hopping across a screen to a precise \nprobability of that dot manifesting as a giant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152160,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space rock burning through our atmosphere \nand flattening a city or a continent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156480,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In our recent episode we talked about the \nOSIRIS-REX mission, which successfully  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160280,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intercepted the asteroid Bennu and returned \na sample to Earth. Bennu is what we call an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165720,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth-crossing asteroid, which means orbital \npath crosses right through Earth’s path. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means it has a potential risk if hitting us \nif that crossing occurs when Earth happens to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also be at the same spot in its orbit. Bennu \nwon’t do that any time soon, although to land  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183520,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the thing we still needed incredibly precise \nknowledge of its trajectory through 3-D space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189800,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which had to be determined from those fuzzy moving \ndots. Now that OSIRIS-REX has finished with Bennu,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s been renamed OSIRIS-APEX, and \nit’ll intercept the asteroid Apophis  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202160,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which has a series of uncomfortably close \napproaches over the next several decades."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207240,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But space is very very big. How is it \npossible to know the location and orbit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214440,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a faint space rock with the precision to \nland on it, or know whether it’ll land on us?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221960,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine that you’re an astronomer doing some \nastronomy of some sort on a big telescope. You  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "notice that an unknown blob moves across the patch \nof sky that you’re observing over the course of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232480,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a few nights. Looks like an asteroid because \nof the speed of motion, and the lack of tail  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238320,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means probably not a comet. You check against \ndatabases of all known solar system bodies and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242520,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s not one of those. So it's new. How\ndo you know whether it’s coming our way?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249400,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From these grainy images, you don’t \nknow much. It could be big, far away,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255200,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and fast, or small, closer, and slower—or \nanything in between. We would say that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261240,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these properties are degenerate—they \nlook the same from this 2-D image."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265760,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now you as an astronomer have seen Don’t Look \nUp and so you know that time is of the essence  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when evaluating a potentially threatening \nobject, so you get to work figuring out its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277320,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit. The first step is to determine the \ntrue location of the object. You know its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283680,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exact angular position—the direction relative to \nEarth, because you know where your telescope was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288760,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pointing in each image and, more importantly, \nyou can calibrate very precisely relative to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the well-known stars in the background. \nBut you have no idea how far away it is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301400,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so we have this degeneracy I mentioned \nbetween close, slow orbits and distant,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fast orbits. Fortunately the problem of \ndetermining an orbit has been solved for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310800,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a couple of hundred years by some of the greats \nof old—Laplace and Gauss in particular. They in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317240,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "turn used the even older work of Johanne Kepler, \nwho provided the first mathematically accurate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323600,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "description of elliptical orbits. So with only \nthree observations, reasonably separated in time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330040,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s possible to determine which \nKeplerian orbit your moving smudge is on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337160,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These days there are improved versions of the \nLaplace and Gauss methods, but the principle is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342640,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same. From your few observations, you manage \nto solve for the six classical Keplerian orbital  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349080,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parameters, which tell you the path the asteroid \nwould follow around the Sun assuming that the only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing affecting that motion was the Sun’s gravity. \nWhich isn’t the case, but I’ll come back to that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360800,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Right away you can figure out some useful stuff. \nThe asteroid has an eccentric orbit that takes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it from midway between the orbits of Earth and \nMars when furthest from the Sun—its aphelion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371160,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to midway between Earth and Venus when closest, \nat perihelion. This means you’ve found a new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377720,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Apollo group asteroid—which means it crosses \nEarth’s orbit and has a “semi-major axis”,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383440,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or average orbital radius, is larger \nthan Earth’s. In contrast the other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387360,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "group of Earth-crossers—the Atens, have \nsmaller semi-major axes than Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Either way, these Earth-crossing asteroids \nare dangerous because they pass through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s path twice during each of its orbits, \nand if that happens while Earth is in that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404920,
    "dDurationMs": 8680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same spot then kablooee. You calm yourself \nby remembering that 2024 YR4 and Apophis,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which did not hit the Earth, were both \nApollos, but then you recall that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418240,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Chelyabinsk and Tunguska objects \nwere also Apollos and they did hit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424040,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But how big a hit would this be? Depends on \nthe object’s size, which you can also now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "estimate. You know that the light you receive \nfrom this asteroid is reflected sunlight,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436520,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and if you assume a typical rocky composition \nyou can guess its albedo—the fraction of light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442360,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reflected. Now that you know its current \ndistance from the Sun and from the Earth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447600,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can compute what surface area it would need \nin order to have the observed brightness. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453920,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in turn gives you the approximate size, with \nsome assumptions about its shape. You estimate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460480,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around 40m in diameter assuming that it’s roughly \nspherical. There’s a very large uncertainty in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467760,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this, so it could actually be as little as half \nor as much as twice that size. If the former,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475280,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s the same size as the one that exploded \nover Chelyabinsk in 2014 with the explosive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equivalent of a smallish thermonuclear bomb. \nBut the upper end of that size range is in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486760,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "realm of the Tunguska impactor that flattened \n2000 square km of Siberian forest in 1908 with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494280,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an explosive power an order of magnitude \nlarger than the Chelyabinsk explosion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499520,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Given a bit of time this size can be determined by \ntaking the spectrum of the object would determine  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506760,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its composition, and so that would give you \na much better constraint on its albedo and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hence size. And observations with a more powerful \ntelescope or radar could resolve the object and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517320,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "literally measure the size—but that’ll have \nto wait for a close approach to the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523200,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now, you have enough information about this \nnew, potentially hazardous object to keep digging."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530400,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now that you know the orbital elements you \ncould just run a simulation of both Earth  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the asteroid’s orbits far, far into the \nvirtual future to see if they ever collide.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540560,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And you know that would be useless. The \nreal solar system is a messy place. Your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "asteroid is going to be tugged off its perfect \nKeplerian orbit by the gravitational fields of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549600,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the planets. It’s also going to be pushed \never so slightly by sunlight—by radiation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554280,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressure. And there can even be a significant \nnudge caused by a sun-heated rock radiating  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561040,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away its own heat in different directions \nas it rotates—that’s the Yarkovsky effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566920,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The only way to understand the \npossible trajectory of this object "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572000,
    "dDurationMs": 8120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is to simulate that future motion step by step \nby step, and at each step figure out the tiny  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580120,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deflections caused by all these influences. The \ngravitational pulls of Venus, Mars, Jupiter,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Saturn and even sometimes the Moon have small \nbut noticeable effects, but the real wild card  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591680,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is Earth’s own influence. When the asteroid makes \na close approach to the Earth, it’s deflected  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598680,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the Earth’s gravitational field, sometimes \ngetting slung into a very different orbit. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603920,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deflection depends strongly on exactly where the \nasteroid passes the Earth on that close approach,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610360,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that’s very hard to predict due to the \nvarious uncertainties. You find that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616320,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "main uncertainties are the effects of solar \nradiation and the Yarkovsky effect due to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621160,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unknown geometry and composition of the object. \nThese have only a tiny influence in the moment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but that influence and so the uncertainty \ncompounds over time. But worse than that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the fact that your starting position and \nvelocity for the asteroid are already uncertain,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639760,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in particular due to the difficulty in \nknowing the exact distance from the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643920,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Due to these uncertainties, there’s no way \nto find the exact path—the best you can do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649080,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is to simulate many, many possible paths \nthat explore the range of the uncertain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parameters—in other words, you need run \na Monte Carlo simulation. Doing that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660880,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you discover a huge range of possible futures for \nthis asteroid. On each close encounter with Earth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668160,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it passes either in front or behind the \nEarth, or, sometimes, straight into it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673240,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But you notice something interesting. Hypothetical \ntrajectories that do end up hitting the Earth tend  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678160,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be deflected onto those deadly paths by the Earth \nitself during a previous close encounter. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "during those close encounters, all of the \nsubsequently cataclysmic trajectories pass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691560,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through small region in space or one of a \nvery few regions. These regions are called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational keyholes, and they can be as small \nas hundreds of meters in diameter.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702640,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If a trajectory misses a keyhole, Earth is safe \nfor the time being, but if it threads one of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708080,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "keyholes for a given close encounter then impact \nat the next close encounter is extremely likely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715040,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Calculating an impact probability is now \nsimplified to calculating the probability  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721560,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of threading a gravitational keyhole. It’s also \ntrue that deflecting a potential impactor is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728080,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simplified to diverting it from passing through a \nkeyhole if you can catch it before that happens.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means you only have to offset its position by \nkilometers at the keyhole, rather than the several  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740400,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thousand km needed to avoid an Earth collision \nafter it already passes a keyhole. In 2022,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747320,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NASA’s DART mission proved we can indeed deflect a \nlarge asteroid by the requisite amount by slamming  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754200,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a mass into it. As long as we do it early enough. \nBut, assuming that keyhole passage happens,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now you can at least calculate with some accuracy \nwhen and where the asteroid will impact the Earth.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766480,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each different keyhole focuses the asteroid \ntowards an impact corridor—a trail across  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773480,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the planet that’s defined by the fairly precise \nlocation that the asteroid crosses our orbit, with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the uncertainty in the crossing time stretching \nthat impact point out into an impact line."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786920,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So now you have a probability of impact \nfor the next few close approaches and a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791520,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rough idea of possible impact locations \nfor each. Further observations over time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796680,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will narrow all of that down as \nthe orbit of the asteroid becomes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800320,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "better known. Ultimately you’ll have a \nvery reliable probability of an impact  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805480,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the next few close encounters. But \nbeyond that the uncertainties blow up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, what did you discover? Do we \nneed to launch the nukes? Organize  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an apocalypse party? I have no idea. \nThis whole scenario is just made up.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Feel free to choose your own ending. But \nthe process I just described is more or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828120,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less what we do to turn a newly noticed \nmoving smudge into an impact probability."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it’s more or less what was done with \nthe most recent scale, 2024 YR4—although  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840040,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in this case by the ATLAS robotic survey, not \nby some astronomer who happened to look at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844960,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right spot. Anyway, as I said, simulations based \non our first set of observations revealed a 3.1%  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852800,
    "dDurationMs": 9440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chance of a December 2032 impact by 2024 \nYR4. The impact corridor was equatorial,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862240,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and included parts of Africa, India and South and \nCentral America But as further observations were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868600,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made, the uncertainty in its orbital parameters \ndropped, and so too did the number of simulated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873360,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trajectories impact the Earth. By March this year \nNASA revised the impact probability to 1 in around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881160,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "5500, while further analysis by ESA set the \nchance to nearly only one in a million. It’s actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889240,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more likely that the rock will hit the Moon in \n2032 than it will the Earth. For the time being,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895880,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2024 YR4 isn’t a threat. Which is good, \nbecause this thing is between 40 and 90  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900840,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meters diameter, which is around the size of \nthe Tunguska impactor. It could make a mess."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908200,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A more perennial threat is the asteroid Apophis, \nwhich is nearly half a kilometer long. Big enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it gets to have a cool name - in this case \nafter the Egyptian god of darkness and chaos,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919240,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which would certainly ensue if Apophis decided \nto descend upon the Earth. If it ever hits,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926200,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it would be with an energy just \nshy of all existing nuclear  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weapons, or several Krakatoa eruptions. That \nmeans regional devastation and tsunamis for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934960,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an ocean hit, as well as some years \nof global cooling from dust and ash  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939240,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the atmosphere. Apophis is too small to \nbe an extinction level impactor—for example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946520,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the object that ended the dinosaurs 66 million \nyears ago was around 20 times larger than apophis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953880,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Still, we should be happy that Apophis won’t \nhit us any time soon. Although when it was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960480,
    "dDurationMs": 9440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first discovered in 2004, early observations and \norbital simulations indicated a 2029 impact chance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969920,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of up to 2.9%. Further observations, including \nradar by Arecibo, slowly downgraded the risk for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977560,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a 2029 impact, instead indicating a possibility of \npassing through an 800m wide gravitational keyhole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985960,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a 2036 impact. Happily, yet more observations \nshow that’s no impact is likely for the next  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993880,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "century. Still, Apophis is a great test case for \nplanetary defense. As I mentioned in the recent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001120,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "episode, that’s one of the reasons the newly \nrenamed OSIRIS-APEX will intercept Apophis on its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now-benign 2029 encounter—in particular to assess \nhow its interaction with Earth’s gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014560,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field changes the structure and rotation of \nthe asteroid—all of which are important for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "future simulations of NEO trajectories \nfollowing a close encounter with Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025240,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As far as we know, there are no giant space rocks \nabout to drop into our atmosphere. The fact that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re still finding new Earth-crossing \nobjects is a bit worrying. However the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037600,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rubin observatory’s LSST survey that starts \nthis year will find many of the missing ones,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043080,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NASA’s Near Earth Object Surveyor to launch \nin 2027 or so is supposed to find at least  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049920,
    "dDurationMs": 11280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "⅔ of objects larger than 140m. In 2005 the US \nCongress mandated NASA to find at least 90% of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061200,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the estimated 15,000 NEOs larger than 140 meters. \nSo, you know, we’re getting there. At least we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069600,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know that when we do spot a new space rock hopping \nacross the grainy starfield of our survey images,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1076520,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we know exactly how to figure out its potentially \nEarth-bound trajectory through space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to DeleteMe for Supporting PBS. DeleteMe \nwas started to help protect its members deal from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1089480,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the work of data brokers – these brokers employ \nweb crawlers to scrape public databases and social  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1094920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "media platforms, cross-reference this information \nwith purchased commercial records, and compile  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1100920,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "complex relational databases of personal data \nthat they then sell. Whether the risk is doxxing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1107120,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "harassment, or identity theft, DeleteMe was \ncreated to help. DeleteMe uses advanced monitoring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1112760,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tools to locate your data across hundreds of \nbroker sites. They compile a custom report showing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1118240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where your information is exposed, and then submit \nformal requests to remove it. They keep scanning  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1123840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to catch reappearances over time. And because \ndata brokers frequently cross-reference related  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1129360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "individuals by enrolling everyone in a family \nplan, DeleteMe membership can help ensure that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1135360,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all your relatives’ personal details are included \nin the scanning and removal process, reducing the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1141560,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "overall risk for the entire family. You can get \n20% off any DeleteMe consumer or family plan  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148040,
    "dDurationMs": 6779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at joindeleteme.com/spacetime with promo code \nspacetime. There’s a link in the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
